WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
STATE OF VIMEO:
Remember Vimeo? Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.720
in the late 2000s and early 2010s, if you searched&nbsp;
for a video on Google, you would see a few results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.320
from Vimeo and DailyMotion. Personally, I always&nbsp;
thought of Vimeo as just a YouTube copycat that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.120
wasn’t nearly as popular but it turns out that&nbsp;
Vimeo was and still is way more popular than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:25.600
you might imagine. In fact, Vimeo has a total of&nbsp;
230 million users from over 190 countries out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.520
which 90 million are active every month. Vimeo&nbsp;
also boasts 60 million content creators and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.600
video catalog that is growing by as much as 900&nbsp;
million videos per year. But, this sizable user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.800
base is pretty much the only thing that Vimeo&nbsp;
has going for them as everything else has turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.640
against them. Basically just one look at their&nbsp;
stock will tell you everything you need to know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.280
Since going public in 2021, it’s been straight&nbsp;
down for Vimeo who has lost 93% of their market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:02.240
value which has dipped down to $625 million. At&nbsp;
this point, they’re almost trading at book value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.200
which isn’t all that much lower. Vimeo has a total&nbsp;
of $610 million worth of assets and $243 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.720
worth of liabilities, so their net assets stand&nbsp;
at $367 million. If we subtract this from Vimeo’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.120
current market valuation, we’ll see that the&nbsp;
business portion of Vimeo is only worth about $250&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:27.480
million. For perspective, YouTube pulls in&nbsp;
that much revenue every 3 days. Seeing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.480
I don’t think you’d be surprised to see that&nbsp;
Vimeo has a bankruptcy probability of 25% over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.640
the next 24 months. Given this bleak situation,&nbsp;
Vimeo has been cutting resources and staff but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.840
this has only made the platform worse which has&nbsp;
turned the community against the company, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.160
last thing they had going for them. So Vimeo is in&nbsp;
this super precarious situation where they still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.000
have nearly a quarter billion users but all they&nbsp;
have to show for it is a company that’s slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.720
bleeding towards bankruptcy and an increasingly&nbsp;
frustrated user base. And you know what the kicker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:05.160
about all of this is? Vimeo was never the YouTube&nbsp;
copycat, they were actually the OG YouTube having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:21.220
launched a couple of months before YouTube.&nbsp;
So, here’s the tragic fate of the OG YouTube.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.320
ACCIDENTAL CREATION:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.000
the story of Vimeo traces back to late 2004 to two&nbsp;
web developers named Jake Lodwick and Zach Klein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.240
Neither of these guys were some sort of tech&nbsp;
wizards that dropped out of Harvard or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.533
They were just modest software engineers working&nbsp;
at a company that they thought was fun and light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:43.880
hearted: CollegeHumor. This was back when people&nbsp;
actually used to watch TV and this one SNL clip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:49.040
about the singer Ashlee Simpson was garnering a&nbsp;
lot of attention. Basically, Ashlee missed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.320
entry on live television which made it painfully&nbsp;
obvious that she was lip syncing. She tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.040
play it off by as no big deal by dancing but this&nbsp;
was unfortunately the end of her singing career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:05.360
The sliver lining of the situation is that this&nbsp;
event is what also gave rise to Vimeo. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.800
the day after this incident, the video clip would&nbsp;
go viral online on all sorts of platforms one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.640
which was CollegeHumor. Jake and Zach saw this&nbsp;
explosion first hand and decided that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.760
be neat to create a platform dedicated to tagging&nbsp;
and sharing short videos just like this. So, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.280
whipped together a site and came up the name Vimeo&nbsp;
which was a combination of the words video and me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.280
The website would go live soon after in November&nbsp;
of 2004 but that was pretty much it. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.400
the website would just organically grow through&nbsp;
word of mouth while Jake and Zach focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.400
helping scale CollegeHumor. It wasn’t till nearly&nbsp;
2 years later till the duo would really look back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.600
at the site. You see, in August of 2006, Barry&nbsp;
Diller, the founder of Fox and USA Broadcasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.440
would make an acquisition offer for the parent&nbsp;
company of College Humor: Connected Ventures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.040
And during the due diligence phase, his team&nbsp;
would come across Vimeo. They didn’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.640
much of it and offered to buy out everything for&nbsp;
a total of $26 million. Just a couple of weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:09.800
later though a news bomb would drop from Google.&nbsp;
They were gonna acquire YouTube for $1.65 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:14.240
Diller immediately realized that he had just&nbsp;
accidentally struck gold and would tell everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.520
at Connected Ventures to go all in on Vimeo with&nbsp;
Jake and Zach leading the effort. By this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.280
YouTube had already become the sort of mass&nbsp;
market video sharing platform, so instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.040
going after popularity, Jake and Zach decided to&nbsp;
go after quality. As such, they would focus their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.560
efforts on better curating content and offering a&nbsp;
better creator and user experience. One of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:39.440
efforts was bring HD streaming to online video.&nbsp;
In fact, in October of 2007, Vimeo would become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.680
the first online video sharing platform to&nbsp;
support HD streaming out of the box. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:50.640
not all was great at Vimeo. As Diller became more&nbsp;
and more concerned about making Vimeo successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.880
he would assert more and more influence over&nbsp;
the company. For example, he would implement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.880
a traditional corporate structure like he had&nbsp;
at his media companies and bring in a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.960
his trusted associates. But this simply created&nbsp;
a larger and larger rift between the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.920
Vimeo team and the new members. Eventually, this&nbsp;
tension would blow over and Jake would decide to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:14.680
leave but before he could, he would actually get&nbsp;
fired in late 2007. Just a couple of months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:29.062
Zach would also leave the company, and ever since&nbsp;
then, it’s only been downhill for the company.

00:05:29.062 --> 00:05:30.560
PIVOT AFTER PIVOT:
With the founders out, Vimeo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.760
would decide to go all in on a complete redesign&nbsp;
of the platform. They wanted the video player to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.200
be the central focus of any page unlike YouTube.&nbsp;
They would also implement a lot of restrictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.920
around what could and could not be uploaded to&nbsp;
Vimeo. Essentially, they only wanted high ROI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:52.280
content which was shorter videos that could appeal&nbsp;
to a broad audience. And this tunnel visioned goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:58.120
led to some pretty odd choices. For example,&nbsp;
starting in 2008, they would ban all gaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:03.040
content. The reasoning was that gaming content&nbsp;
tended to be long and take up a lot of resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:09.320
while only appealing to a niche group of returning&nbsp;
viewers. They did eventually lift this ban in 2014&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:13.560
but this gave YouTube a full 6 years to blow&nbsp;
up in all sorts of niches that Vimeo creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:19.000
couldn’t even post about. By this point, there was&nbsp;
no catching up to YouTube, so Vimeo would double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.360
down on trying to appeal to indie filmmakers and&nbsp;
creatives. For example, in 2010, they would launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.760
the Vimeo Festival and Awards Program. This was&nbsp;
basically a competition for rising filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.600
to show off their work and gain exposure. Around&nbsp;
the same tim, they would introduce 4K downloading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:39.680
support and in 2015, they would launch 4K&nbsp;
streaming support. Vimeo also boasted far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.160
less compression than YouTube in order to better&nbsp;
protect the creator’s original vision. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.720
these efforts were able to build a strong&nbsp;
base of creatives, Vimeo was aiming for much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.440
more than that. So, it late 2016, they announced&nbsp;
their plans to spend tens of millions of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:00.360
on creating original in house content to compete&nbsp;
against Netflix. If this sounds like a crazy out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:05.640
there idea, it was and thankfully, they didn’t go&nbsp;
too far off the deep end before calling it quits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:12.000
just one year later. Vimeo was back to ground&nbsp;
zero. They needed a new way to pivot and continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.120
growing but with competing against YouTube&nbsp;
and Netflix directly being off the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.880
they really had no direction until they talked&nbsp;
to a rising leader named Anjali Sud. Sud was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.800
U Penn and Harvard graduate that was currently&nbsp;
servering as the head of global marketing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.000
Vimeo. And Sud had a completely different vision&nbsp;
for Vimeo. Instead of being a consumer business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.200
what if they became an enterprise busines. There&nbsp;
were a lot of businesses that were left behind by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:43.200
the age of the internet. So, what if they helped&nbsp;
these businesses create promotional videos and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.400
feedback before sharing it online? With not much&nbsp;
to lose, Vimeo’s board would approve the pivot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:55.200
place Sud as the new CEO of Vimeo in July of 2017.&nbsp;
Sud would go onto rebrand Vimeo as a SAAS platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.200
and release a slew of editing and review tools.&nbsp;
She would also complete multiple acquisitions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:06.160
brought in tens of millions of new users to Vimeo.&nbsp;
For example, in early 2019, she purchased Magisto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:11.520
an AI video creation service with over a&nbsp;
hundred million users. And in late 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.840
Vimeo would purchase yet another AI based video&nbsp;
creation platform called Wibbitz. While this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:22.880
a rather unexpected direction for Vimeo, it seemed&nbsp;
like things were paying off. Enterprise customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:28.160
quickly become Vimeo’s fastest growing segment&nbsp;
accounting for the majority of company’s revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.880
Their user count was also exploding thanks to the&nbsp;
pandemic reaching over 200 million by late 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:47.780
But the reality was the Vimeo was a lot more&nbsp;
fragile than any of these numbers suggested.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:50.000
THE GREAT COLLAPSE:
One key point that we have yet to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.920
is how exactly Vimeo makes money which turns out&nbsp;
to be the company’s biggest shortfall. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.320
you would think that Vimeo makes money off of&nbsp;
ads just like any other video sharing or social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:06.840
media platform. But, Vimeo actually shows no ads&nbsp;
at all. Given that Vimeo targetted a higher end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:11.680
user base of film makers and creatives, they&nbsp;
didn’t want to bog down the platform with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.760
bunch of ads. And as such, the only way that&nbsp;
Vimeo makes money is from creators and viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.720
directly paying them. Creators and businesses&nbsp;
have to pay a fee based on how many videos they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.280
upload and what features they want. And Vimeo&nbsp;
also gets a 10% commission on any money that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:33.680
users are willing to support creators with.&nbsp;
It’s a pretty straight forward model but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:39.800
not all that profitable. In fact, despite having&nbsp;
over 200 million users, Vimeo was only pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:46.040
in a little more than $400 million per year or&nbsp;
barely $2 per user per year. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:52.440
YouTube pulls in closer to $11 per user per year&nbsp;
and Facebook pulls in over $20 per user per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.640
And given that video storage isn’t exactly&nbsp;
cheap especially uncompressed video storage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:01.800
Vimeo has never really been profitable.&nbsp;
Throughout much of Sud’s leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.520
she just raised a bunch of money to make up&nbsp;
for this disparity. For example, in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:14.280
she raised $150 million and 2021 she raised $300&nbsp;
million. But all of this peaked in mid 2021 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:20.120
she decided to take the company public at a $10&nbsp;
billion valuation. The harsh reality was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:25.040
investors simply didn’t care about investing in a&nbsp;
second tier video streaming platform, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:30.240
one that was losing money when they could just&nbsp;
invest in Google or Netflix. On IPO day itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:36.960
Vimeo would fall 13% and it’s only been downhill&nbsp;
since then. As such, Vimeo has very much started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.320
focusing on turning a profit which has led to&nbsp;
some pretty unpopular decisions. On one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.080
they’ve been cutting resources which has led&nbsp;
to widespread buffering issues. It also seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:51.760
that Vimeo’s UI is starting to feel dated and&nbsp;
their video curation has very much fallen behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.680
industry standards something that they used&nbsp;
to lead. But likely their most controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:02.160
decision by far was jacking up prices for content&nbsp;
creators, especially their most popular and active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:07.320
creators. In some cases, creators were expected&nbsp;
to pay thousands of dollars just to keep their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:12.440
content on Vimeo. While extremely unpopular&nbsp;
though, these drastic measures have allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:17.800
to company to barely become profitable. They’re&nbsp;
currently pulling in about $8 million per year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:24.120
but at what cost? They’re basically stuck in&nbsp;
no mans land with a bunch of angry users and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:29.920
no real way to move forward. Sud has since been&nbsp;
forced to step down and has been replaced by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:36.120
long time cloud executive named Adam Gross. Given&nbsp;
that Vimeo has been able to achieve profitability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:43.200
I don’t seem them going under anytime per se&nbsp;
but it’s also hard to see a bright future ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:49.200
for Vimeo either which is quite a shame given that&nbsp;
they were the OG video streaming platform. YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:54.800
seems to be facing their own crisis with ads and&nbsp;
short form hell. Check out this video learn more.

